      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( 11t - 36 \\) must be divisible by 100. We start by finding the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. In other words, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. First, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 11 and 100:\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\nThe gcd is 1, and we can work backwards to express 1 as a combination of 11 and 100:\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\nThis shows that the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since \\(-9\\) is negative, we convert it to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can solve for \\( t \\) by multiplying both sides of the original congruence by 91:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nFirst, we compute \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\nNext, we find the remainder when 3276 is divided by 100:\n\\[\n3276 \\div 100 = 32 \\text{ remainder } 76\n\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\nThus, \\( t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\). Since \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, the only possible value is \\( t = 76 \\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\nt = \\boxed{76}\n\\]",
